{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464052155,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUxNTYzOTk0MDA=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/464","origin":"github"},"title":"re-invocation of create-publication-target... -- API/usecase","message":"So we have published previous crawl of the openfmri repos, but then added more submodules.  I guess now I need to `create-publication-target` for new submodules.  Ideally I should be able just to point to the name of the remote (known to \"super-dataset\") and datalad, based on URL already known to super-dataset should just go and create new placeholders for submodules where needed.  But ATM API forces to provide URL again:\n\n```\n$\u003e datalad create-publication-target-sshwebserver\nusage: datalad create-publication-target-sshwebserver [--version] [-h]\n                                                      [-l {critical,error,warning,info,debug,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9}]\n                                                      [-p {condor}]\n                                                      [--target-dir TARGET_DIR]\n                                                      [--target-url [TARGET_URL]]\n                                                      [--target-pushurl TARGET_PUSHURL]\n                                                      [--dataset DATASET]\n                                                      [--recursive] [--force]\n                                                      [--shared SHARED]\n                                                      sshurl [target]\n```\n\nso `sshurl` is not optional (while target is).  I wonder if we should\n1. store any of the custom options (`--shared`, `--target-url`,...) within git options for that remote (e.g. with `datalad-` prefix similarly to as annex does), so subsequent invocations could reuse them.  It would be unreasonable to require remembering them\n2. make `sshurl` an optional positional argument, while `target` -- an option (`--remote` or `--target` if you want to keep moving away from git terminology).  Then I could probably just invoke smth like `datalad create-publication-target --remote datalad-public` without worrying to remember originally used option(s)","files":null}]}